WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
Parsing gives the program fields.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.440
Validation decides whether those fields are acceptable.

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.820
A value can be syntactically present and still be wrong for the program's purpose.

4
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.840
Defensive configuration parsers validate each field against the type and context where it will be used.

5
00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.360
Booleans should have accepted forms.

6
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:34.380
Integers, sizes, durations, ports, percentages, and counts should check conversion success, leftover text, range limits, sign expectations, units, and overflow concerns in later calculations.

7
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.320
A successful conversion is only the beginning of numeric validation.

8
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.840
Enumerations and identifiers should be restricted to allowed values.

9
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:53.500
A mode setting, log level, algorithm choice, feature flag, or resource name should not silently accept arbitrary text if only a small set of values is meaningful.

10
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.240
Unknown values should be rejected or handled by a documented compatibility rule.

11
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.320
Lists need item-by-item validation.

12
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:09.660
The parser should define separators, empty items, duplicate items, maximum item count, and whether ordering matters.

13
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.720
One invalid item should not be silently skipped unless the configuration contract explicitly permits partial acceptance.

14
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.400
Path-like strings, names, and resource identifiers need format rules tied to their use.

15
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:30.540
They may need length checks, character rules, location expectations, or normalization before use.

16
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.760
The validation rule should be close to the field definition rather than scattered across later consumers.

17
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.640
Context matters most.

18
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:48.860
A value may be valid text and valid syntax but still unacceptable for a field because it would exceed a resource budget, conflict with another setting, or choose a disabled feature.

19
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.520
Validation should confirm that configured values are meaningful for the final runtime state.
